# FIXED DEFECT, 2026-08-16 (panel 081 R4). Symptom: a C `char[N]` struct member
# could not be bound at ANY of Heroes' eight integer widths — `ffi_field_type`
# eight times over — so `uname()` was unreachable at any price, and its `partial`
# escape hatch was refused as `empty_record` because `struct utsname` is nothing
# but `char` arrays. Cause: `emit/extern_field.rs` asked **class, width and sign**
# of a scalar integer (panel 064) and **`_Generic` type identity** of an array
# element, and C's `char` is a THIRD type, distinct from both `signed char` and
# `unsigned char` — so `&t.name` is `char (*)[4]` and never matches
# `int8_t (*)[4]`. Panel 064's own argument, one type late: C's integer ABI is
# width and sign, and C's type identity is finer than its ABI.
#
# Scale, from a clang AST walk over eleven POSIX headers plus raylib, SDL3,
# sqlite3, zlib and curl: 26 of 81 fixed-array struct fields are `char[N]` — a
# third of them. CLAUDE.md §12 asks the FFI to be complete as well as bug-proof,
# and "a library Heroes cannot bind is a library the author must leave C code
# around for". Named after the defect, per Go's `test/fixedbugs` (CLAUDE.md §9).
#
# What the repair must NOT relax, all four measured before this case was written:
#   u8[4]  against char[4]  -> refused, `char` is signed here
#   i16[4] against char[4]  -> refused, wrong width
#   i8[8]  against char[4]  -> refused, wrong length
#   i8[2]  against char[4]  -> refused, wrong length
# and a flexible array member (`char data[]`) keeps panel 079's own message,
# `ffi_flexible_array_member`, rather than being told to correct a length that
# does not exist. The length conjunct is asked of the field's OWN element type
# (`__typeof__(place[0])[n]`), which is what makes it exact and sign-agnostic at
# once: it decides the count and never re-litigates the element.
#
# The header is local rather than `<sys/utsname.h>`: Windows has no `utsname`,
# and a real one needs a 256-element literal to construct, which is the separate
# gap panel 081 recorded and this case does not claim to close.

extern "ffi-a-char-array-member.h"
    record Tag
        name: i8[4]
        id: i32
    function tag_first(t: Tag) -> i32

function main()
    # 68 is `D`. Nothing here is about the value — it is about the field binding
    # at all, which it did not before today.
    t = Tag(name: [68, 0, 0, 0], id: 7)
    print(t.tag_first().to_i64().must())
    print(t.name[0].to_i64().must(), " ", t.id.to_i64().must())
